    "innovation": "This paper introduces the concept that Pine Island Bay is the \"weak underbelly\" of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, synthesizing field observations to propose a collapse scenario driven by the unbuttressed Thwaites and Pine Island Glaciers, which is supported by a numerical model.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet disintegration model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "grounding-line retreat mechanism",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Our CLIMAP ice-sheet disintegration model coupled a grounding-line retreat mechanism published by Thomas (1977) to theoretical ice-stream profiles I had derived for our CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "sliding law of glacial ice",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The theoretical variation of To along an ice stream of length Is is To = k[1-f{1-cos(\u03c0x/Is)}]4m/(2m+1)TM (6) where k quantifies sheet-flow converging to become stream-flow, m is the viscoplastic exponent in the sliding law ux = (To/B)m of glacial ice, T\u2122 is the basal shear stress for sheet-flow over a thawed bed, To = TM over the region x > 2Is of sheet-flow, x = Is at the grounding line if a floating ice tongue forms, x = o at the grounding line if a grounded ice lobe forms, f = o for sheet-flow, f = 1 for stream- flow, and o <f < 1 for lobate-flow at the ice margin (Hughes, 1981). Substituting Equation (6) into Equation (3) produces concave ice-stream profiles that can be fitted to Thwaites and Pine Island Glaciers, once their ice thicknesses and surface slopes are mapped by radio echo-sounding.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "isostatic sinking of the bed",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Equation (12) constructs surface profiles that have been lowered by isostatic sinking of the bed under the ice sheet. In the original CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model, ice-sheet profiles were constructed above the present-day undepressed bed using Equation (3), and then the bed was isostatically depressed at each Ax step and a new surface profile was constructed (Hughes and others, 1981).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "exponential isostatic rebound of bedrock",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Balancing gravity forces for exponential isostatic rebound of bedrock having density pr gives r = h''/h' = ro exp(\u2014t/to) (14) where ro = \u03c1\u03b9/(PR-PI) for full isostatic equilibrium, to is the time constant, ice thickness h' above the flotation elevation of marine ice isostatically depresses the bed an amount h\", full isostatic equilibrium before rebound gives r = ro at t = o, and full isostatic equilibrium after rebound gives r = o att = \u221e. Equation (3) will reconstruct the present-day West Antarctic ice sheet over the present-day bed. Equation (12) will reconstruct the present-day West Antarctic ice sheet over the fully rebounded bed, provided that the present-day bed is in isostatic equilibrium.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "ice surface elevation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "We evaluated ux using our CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model. In that model, the ice surface elevation above sea-level at Ax step i+ 1 measured from the ice-sheet margin along a surface flow line is hi+1=hi+[To/(h-hr)]\u00a1\u2206x/pig (3)",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "basal shear stress",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "where h, hr, and 70 are the ice surface elevation, bed elevation, and basal shear stress at step i, Ax is the step length, and i is an integer. Taking i = o at the calving front of an ice shelf, \u03c4\u03bf = o for all Ax steps from the calving front to the grounding line and 70 is determined from theory for all Ax steps from the grounding line to the ice divide.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet disintegration model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "grounding line retreat rate",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "More and better field data will justify improvements in our CLIMAP ice-sheet disintegration model. The ability of an ice shelf to buttress ice streams can be modelled. In our model, the grounding line retreat rate of an ice stream having a floating terminus is Ux = (\u03c1\u03c9/\u03c1\u03b9) \u03c5z+(\u03b1-\u03b2) ux-a-hezz / \u03b1-\u03b2(1-\u03c1\u03c9/\u03c1\u03b9)",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet disintegration model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "APPLIES_TO_REALM",
            "target_entity_instance": "landIce",
            "target_entity_type": "Realm",
            "paragraph": "Our first test of the concept that Pine Island Bay is the weak underbelly of the West Antarctic ice sheet was made using the numerical model of ice-sheet disintegration that I developed in the summer of 1977 for the CLIMAP maximum interglaciation experiment (Stuiver and others, 1981).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "APPLIES_TO_REALM",
            "target_entity_instance": "landIce",
            "target_entity_type": "Realm",
            "paragraph": "In our original CLIMAP ice-sheet reconstruction model, theoretical basal shear-stress variations TF and TM for sheet-flow over frozen and thawed beds were calculated for ice sheets having an accumulation zone over their entire surface (Hughes, 1981).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "surges of Thwaites and Pine Island Glaciers",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pine Island Bay",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Possible collapse of the West Antarctic ice sheet by surges of Thwaites and Pine Island Glaciers into the Pine Island Bay polynya of the Amundsen Sea was a subject addressed in papers by Lingle and Clark (1979) and Thomas (1979), and in abstracts by Denton and others (1979) and Hughes (1979), that were published in Vol. 24, No. go of the Journal of Glaciology.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
